## Why This Is Asked
This question tests advanced tree DP with edge deletions and a non-trivial objective—maximizing the sum of component maxima. It requires careful state design, knapsack-style merging, and the ability to reconstruct the solution from computed values.

## Key Concepts
- **Tree DP with edge cuts**: Model each edge cut as separating a subtree into a new component
- **Knapsack-like merging over children**: Combine subproblems using convolution-style DP
- **Maintaining component maxima**: Track both the sum (dp) and the current component's max weight (mx)

## Code Example
```javascript
function solve(n, edges, weights, k) {
    const children = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
    for (const [u, v] of edges) children[u - 1].push(v - 1);

    const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(-1e9));
    const mx = Array.from({ length: n }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(-1e9));
    const parent = Array(n).fill(-1);

    function dfs(u) {
        dp[u][0] = 0;
        mx[u][0] = weights[u];
        for (const v of children[u]) {
            parent[v] = u;
            dfs(v);
            for (let t = k; t >= 0; t--) {
                const ndp = new Array(k + 1).fill(-1e9);
                const nmx = new Array(k + 1).fill(-1e9);
                for (let t1 = 0; t1 <= t; t1++) {
                    const t2 = t - t1;
                    // cut edge
                    ndp[t] = Math.max(ndp[t], dp[u][t1] + dp[v][t2] + mx[v][t2]);
                    nmx[t] = Math.max(nmx[t], mx[u][t1]);
                    // keep edge
                    ndp[t] = Math.max(ndp[t], dp[u][t1] + dp[v][t2]);
                    nmx[t] = Math.max(nmx[t], Math.max(mx[u][t1], mx[v][t2]));
                }
                dp[u] = ndp;
                mx[u] = nmx;
            }
        }
    }

    dfs(0);
    return { maxSum: dp[0][k], edgesToCut: [] }; // reconstruct edges by backtracking decisions
}
```

## Common Pitfalls
- **Forgetting to reset mx when cutting**: When an edge is cut, the parent component's max weight remains unchanged
- **Incorrect knapsack direction**: Always iterate cuts in descending order to avoid reusing the same child multiple times
- **Off-by-one in component counting**: k edge cuts always produce k+1 components

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP on subsequences with transition costs; requires deriving a 2D DP with O(n^2 m) time and backpointers. Edge cases: m=1 yields 0. Consider tie-breaking on reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on subsequences; 2D states; O(n^2 m) time; backtracking via parents; rolling arrays for space.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostSubseq(A, m){
  const n = A.length;
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, ()=>Array(m+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, ()=>Array(m+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0; i<n; i++) dp[i][1] = 0;
  for(let i=0; i<n; i++){
    for(let t=2; t<=m; t++){
      for(let p=0; p<i; p++){
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + Math.abs(A[i]-A[p]);
        if(val < dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = INF, end = -1;
  for(let i=0; i<n; i++){ if(dp[i][m] < best){ best = dp[i][m]; end = i; } }
  const idx = []; let t=m, cur = end;
  while(t >= 1){ idx.push(cur); cur = par[cur][t]; t--; }
  idx.reverse();
  return {cost: best, indices: idx};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests dynamic programming over non-overlapping subarrays where block value depends on internal structure, requiring precomputation and reconstruction techniques.

## Key Concepts

- Non-overlapping k blocks in increasing order
- Precomputation of per-block value (peaks inside)
- DP with inner maximization over l
- Reconstruction pointers; time/space trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxValueSubarrays(A, k, F, P) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const peaks = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));

  // Precompute peaks
  for (let i = 1; i < n-1; i++) {
    if (A[i-1] < A[i] && A[i] > A[i+1]) {
      for (let l = 0; l <= i; l++) {
        for (let r = i; r < n; r++) {
          peaks[l][r]++;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const val = (l, r) => pref[r+1] - pref[l] - F - P * peaks[l][r];

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        if (dp[l-1][t-1] + val(l-1, i-1) > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[l-1][t-1] + val(l-1, i-1);
          parent[i][t] = l-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct
  const blocks = [];
  let cur = n, curT = k;
  while (curT > 0 && parent[cur][curT] !== -1) {
    const start = parent[cur][curT];
    blocks.unshift([start, cur-1]);
    cur = start;
    curT--;
  }

  return { max: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Complexity

- Time: O(n²k) due to nested loops for peaks, DP, and reconstruction
- Space: O(nk) for DP table and parent pointers, reducible to O(n) with rolling arrays

